From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: linux-a9 <linux-a9@localhost>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 09:56:33 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 07/20] media: apple: vxd: fix the GART for 16 KiB pages and DRAM
 above 4 GiB

The GART could not have mapped anything correctly on the device. Three
defects, all invisible in the 4 KiB-page QEMU smoke where DRAM sits
below 4 GiB:

- VXD_GART_PTE() cast the physical address to u32 before shifting.
  AppleD5500::initialize_gart computes ((PA >> 8) & 0xfffffff0) | 1 on
  the full 40-bit address.  The j71t DRAM starts at 0x8_0000_0000
  (device run 1: "dir 0x803758000"), so every directory and page-table
  entry pointed 32 GiB below DRAM.
- GART pages are 4 KiB, but map/unmap and the directory stepped by
  PAGE_SIZE, which is 16 KiB on the device kernel.  Directory entry i
  pointed at pts + i * 16 KiB while the CPU filled the tables 4 KiB
  apart, and a mapping wrote one entry per 16 KiB, so three quarters of
  the firmware image resolved to the dummy page.
- unmap recomputed the device VA from the DMA address and ignored
  apple_vxd.va_offset, so it cleared the identity slots and left the
  offset mapping live.

The walk now always steps VXD_GART_PAGE_SIZE (4 KiB), the page tables
are num_pts consecutive 4 KiB tables (2 MiB instead of 8 MiB on the
device), and the entry macro matches Apple's arithmetic.  A mapping is
validated before the first entry is written, so a bad request no longer
leaves a partial mapping behind.  Live mappings are tracked on the GART
list, and a dirty flag records changes for the decode submit path (Apple
rewrites 0x1008 = 0xf00 before the next submit after a GART change).

The directory is now allocated 64 KiB aligned, as Apple does
(withOptions(0x403, 0x1000, alignment 0x10000)).  The decode message
carries (MMU_DIR_ADR & ~0xff) | hwContextID in a single word.  The
AppleD5500 submit path builds that word from the same directory
descriptor (+0x27a8) it programs into 0x1020, so the low 16 address
bits have to be zero.  The DMA mask is now 40 bits, the physical range
a GART entry can hold.

Signed-off-by: linux-a9 <linux-a9@localhost>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <maintainer@example.invalid>
---
